00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:08.160
Oh, wow, almost all of you.

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:09.240
OK, cool.

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.550
So think about something
like activity detection

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:12.870
if you're writing code.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:16.260
Like, say, take,
for example, I have

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.792
a sensor that's giving me the
speed at which I'm moving.

00:04:18.792 --> 00:04:19.959
So I can say, you know what?

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:22.140
If my speed is less
than four miles an hour,

00:04:22.140 --> 00:04:24.960
I can set my status as
walking in code like this.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:28.643
But now I want to
add running to that.

00:04:28.643 --> 00:04:30.810
So now it's like, OK, if
my speed is less than four,

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:32.722
then I'm walking;
otherwise I'm running.

00:04:32.722 --> 00:04:34.680
And now let's say I want
to add biking to that.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.990
So if my speed is less
than four, I'm walking,

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.720
if it's less than 12, I'm
running; otherwise, I'm biking.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:42.420
It's a basic algorithm
and it kind of works.

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:43.950
But then my boss,
who loves golf,

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:45.400
