00:04:49.027 --> 00:04:51.110
When I hit a golf ball it,
goes about three yards,

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:51.990
so I walk this far.

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.820
But when Ibrahim
hits a golf ball,

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:55.725
he will hit it,
what, 200 yards, 250?

00:04:55.725 --> 00:04:57.477
IBRAHIM ULUKAYA: Larry!

00:04:57.477 --> 00:04:59.310
LAURENCE MORONEY: So
it's like the algorithm

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:01.018
for him would be a
lot different from me,

00:05:01.018 --> 00:05:04.030
and we end up not being able
to write a reliable activity

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:04.530
detection.

00:05:04.530 --> 00:05:07.110
And I know this is a very
naive algorithm based on speed,

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:09.190
but hopefully it gets
the point across.

00:05:09.190 --> 00:05:11.250
So how do we solve
something like this?

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.530
Well, that's the idea
behind machine learning

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:16.030
and how we like to think of
machine learning at Google.

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:18.630
So first of all, if we talk
about traditional programming,

00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:21.630
traditional programming is you
express rules in a programming

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:26.250
language, maybe Java maybe C++,
maybe JavaScript, whatever.

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.890
Those roles will act on
data, and then out of that,

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:29.910
you'll get answers.

00:05:29.910 --> 00:05:32.070
Like I had earlier on, if
speed is less than four,

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:34.350
then I'm walking, that's
traditional programming

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:37.080
and as high level as I
can actually draw it.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:38.850
So machine learning,
the idea behind that,

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:40.867
is we flip the
axes on this chart

00:05:40.867 --> 00:05:42.450
and we end up saying,
well, what if we

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.730
have a whole bunch
of answers, and then

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.220
we have data associated
with those answers,

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:49.380
and then we have a
machine actually learn

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:52.260
the rules itself
as opposed to me

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:55.170
as a programmer trying
to express those rules.

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:57.600
Then some of the limited
scenarios and some

00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:59.160
of the difficulties
in a scenario,

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:01.410
like activity detection
that I mentioned,

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:03.280
they could be fixed
by saying, I can

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:05.050
get a whole bunch
of people walking,

00:06:05.050 --> 00:06:07.450
and I can say this is
what walking looks like.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:08.950
I can get a bunch
of people running,

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:10.780
and say this is what
running looks like,

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:13.690
this is what biking looks
like, and, yeah, sort of this

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:15.470
is what golfing looks like.

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:17.140
So the idea behind
machine learning

00:06:17.140 --> 00:06:18.970
is if I get all
of that data, then

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:20.710
it's just really
as simple as I can

00:06:20.710 --> 00:06:23.110
have a computer start
sifting through this data

00:06:23.110 --> 00:06:26.200
and spot the patterns that
make a commonality for walking,

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:28.820
spot the patterns that make
a commonality for running,

00:06:28.820 --> 00:06:30.625
biking, golfing, all
that kind of stuff.

00:06:30.625 --> 00:06:32.500
So the framework that
we've built around this

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:33.375
is called TensorFlow.

00:06:33.375 --> 00:06:35.800
And TensorFlow is
what I work on.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:36.330
Whoops.

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:37.663
Slightly broken animation there.

00:06:37.663 --> 00:06:39.070
You didn't see that.

00:06:39.070 --> 00:06:42.582
So going back to this diagram
that I showed you earlier on,

00:06:42.582 --> 00:06:44.040
machine learning,
we think about it

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:46.800
as we're feeding in the answers,
we're feeding in the data.

00:06:46.800 --> 00:06:49.410
We write some code in a
framework like TensorFlow

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:51.330
that matches the
answers to the data

00:06:51.330 --> 00:06:53.790
and then infers the
rules out of that.

00:06:53.790 --> 00:06:56.290
What that will do is build
what we call a model.

00:06:56.290 --> 00:06:58.710
And a model is effectively
a neural network.

00:06:58.710 --> 00:07:01.113
It's a really badly named
technology, between you and me,

00:07:01.113 --> 00:07:03.030
because it's got nothing
to do with the brain,

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:05.928
and sometimes it makes you think
it's a lot smarter than it is.

00:07:05.928 --> 00:07:07.470
But if you think of
a neural network,

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:10.070
it's basically a bunch of
interconnected functions,

00:07:10.070 --> 00:07:11.820
and each of these
interconnected functions

00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:14.640
calculate a probability
based on their inputs.

00:07:14.640 --> 00:07:17.310
But what that will end
up giving us is a model.

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:20.220
And then at runtime, we're going
to pass data into the model,

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:23.950
and it will give us an inference
or a prediction out of that.

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:26.700
So now, again, when I was
thinking of my activity

00:07:26.700 --> 00:07:28.530
detection, I've
trained it on a lot

00:07:28.530 --> 00:07:31.230
of people saying what it
looks like to walk, run, bike,

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:32.160
and golf.

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:34.590
Now I'm going to pass it a
bunch of data, and it will say,

00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:36.630
I think this looks like walking.

00:07:36.630 --> 00:07:39.270
Instead of me writing
code, this inference

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:42.280
is happening based on that
data pattern matching.

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:45.610
So building these models is
what we call the training phase.

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:48.300
So as we're creating a model,
as we're passing it the data,

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:50.490
as we're making a neural
network to sift through

00:07:50.490 --> 00:07:52.620
that data to come up
with the patterns that

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:55.650
determine one thing over
another, we call that training.

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:58.720
And then at runtime,
we call that inference.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:01.080
So the idea now
is like, OK, now I

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.900
kind of what machine
learning's all about.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:04.720
What do I do?

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:06.570
Where should I run my inference?

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:09.970
And Ibrahim is going to
tell us all about that.

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:12.000
IBRAHIM ULUKAYA: So now
you have the question,

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.740
where should I do my inference?

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:16.400
We have two options here.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:21.450
We can do the inference on the
Cloud or locally on the device.

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:23.380
Why I might do it in the Cloud?

00:08:23.380 --> 00:08:27.110
If you want to integrate into
an existing Cloud service,

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:30.560
or you may want to use
large, complex models that

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:33.830
wouldn't be able to
fit in a mobile device,

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:36.080
or you want to use
aggregate learning

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:39.000
from a wide variety of sources.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.770
And using the Cloud,
you can actually

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:43.559
minimize the impact
on mobile battery

00:08:43.559 --> 00:08:48.310
and support less
performance, older devices.

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:51.160
What about the
case for on device?

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:54.610
With on device, you'll be
able to keep the data local

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:57.560
and avoid round trip latency.

00:08:57.560 --> 00:09:00.780
This is particularly useful
on real-time applications

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:03.880
where you want to
process multiple frames

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:05.930
in a quick succession.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:08.480
And sometimes, you want
your app to work even

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:11.270
when it's disconnected,
and you want

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:15.200
to reach users without reliable
or affordable connectivity.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:17.090
And this time, you will be able.

00:09:17.090 --> 00:09:21.020
On device is a much
better inference type.

00:09:21.020 --> 00:09:22.947
And Lawrence will go
through the options

00:09:22.947 --> 00:09:24.030
that Google provides here.

00:09:24.030 --> 00:09:25.333
LAURENCE MORONEY: Thanks.

00:09:25.333 --> 00:09:27.000
So again, thinking
about your inference,

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:27.947
you've built a model.

00:09:27.947 --> 00:09:29.280
You want to run inference on it.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:31.090
And as Abraham said,
there's two choices.

00:09:31.090 --> 00:09:32.548
You can either run
it in the Cloud,

00:09:32.548 --> 00:09:33.715
or you can run it on device.

00:09:33.715 --> 00:09:35.881
So let's first look at if
you're going to be running

00:09:35.881 --> 00:09:37.050
your inference in the Cloud.

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:38.967
So you're not deploying
a model to the device.

00:09:38.967 --> 00:09:41.467
Here's where you see all the
Cloud-based inference and stuff

00:09:41.467 --> 00:09:43.560
that you've been seeing
at this conference today.

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:47.100
So Cloud-based inference from
Google, there's three pillars.

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:50.430
There's computer vision-based
stuff that we call sights.

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:53.160
There's language-based stuff
for translation and NLP.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:55.230
And then there's
conversational-based stuff.

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:57.960
So these are models that
have been built by Google

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:00.490
and services that have
been provided by Google.

00:10:00.490 --> 00:10:02.410
So if you're meeting
these three scenarios,

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:04.160
and you want to run
inference in the Cloud

00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:06.780
on these three scenarios,
it's available to you.

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:09.210
Now, in addition to that,
the models that we've built,

00:10:09.210 --> 00:10:10.898
of course, can be customized.

00:10:10.898 --> 00:10:13.440
So the customization of these
models come through the product

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:14.970
called AutoML.

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:17.100
So you see, like,
AutoML Vision allows

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.320
you to retrain Google's
Computer Vision

00:10:19.320 --> 00:10:22.120
model to recognize your needs.

00:10:22.120 --> 00:10:24.647
For example, you're a
manufacturer who makes widgets.

00:10:24.647 --> 00:10:26.730
And you want some kind of
computer vision solution

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:28.260
that recognizes widgets.

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:31.680
You could use AutoML to retrain
it based on your widgets.

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:33.580
Or in language,
for example, if you

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:36.630
want to do translation, so
translation from language

00:10:36.630 --> 00:10:39.140
to language with
machine translation,

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:40.260
it can be pretty good.

00:10:40.260 --> 00:10:41.950
It's getting better
all the time,

00:10:41.950 --> 00:10:44.070
but it's trained on
a very general set.

00:10:44.070 --> 00:10:45.670
Again, you might
be a manufacturer

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:48.810
of building something that has
a specific language associated

00:10:48.810 --> 00:10:49.470
with it.

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:51.750
Maybe your widgets
have particular things

00:10:51.750 --> 00:10:53.760
associated with the
widgets themselves

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:55.360
that don't translate properly.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:10:58.320
You could retrain the
machine translation models

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:00.690
based on your specific domain.

00:11:00.690 --> 00:11:02.530
That's what AutoML is all about.

00:11:02.530 --> 00:11:04.890
So that's Cloud-based
inference for these three

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:06.790
specific scenarios.

00:11:06.790 --> 00:11:09.300
But then we always
get the question, OK.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:11.880
It's great if it's
sights, if it's language,

00:11:11.880 --> 00:11:13.215
or if it's conversational.

00:11:13.215 --> 00:11:15.090
But what if I'm doing
something that's unique

00:11:15.090 --> 00:11:16.260
and something different?

00:11:16.260 --> 00:11:18.300
For example, somebody
asked me recently

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:20.430
about they wanted to do
network configuration.

00:11:20.430 --> 00:11:22.260
And they've these
massive networks.

00:11:22.260 --> 00:11:25.170
And right now, all of the
configuration on these networks

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:25.980
is done manually.

00:11:25.980 --> 00:11:27.897
Wouldn't it be great if
they could see traffic

00:11:27.897 --> 00:11:30.810
on these networks and then use
software-defined networking

00:11:30.810 --> 00:11:32.970
to automatically configure
their networks based

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:34.440
on a learned behavior?

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:37.822
So there's a great example
of a custom ML model.

00:11:37.822 --> 00:11:39.280
So the question
then becomes, well,

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:41.160
if I have a custom
ML model and I

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:44.100
want to host that in
the Cloud, what do I do?

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:46.320
So typically, there's
three main ways

00:11:46.320 --> 00:11:48.240
that you build models
for machine learning.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:50.328
Number one is a
pretrained model.

00:11:50.328 --> 00:11:52.620
A little bit like I just
mentioned some of the services

00:11:52.620 --> 00:11:54.078
that we have in
the Cloud, somebody

00:11:54.078 --> 00:11:55.380
has built a model for you.

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:57.060
Those services I
showed earlier on

00:11:57.060 --> 00:11:58.830
are models that Google
has built for you.

00:11:58.830 --> 00:12:00.847
But we're not the only
people who build models.

00:12:00.847 --> 00:12:03.180
So the idea is that there are
a ton of pretrained models

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:04.930
out there that you
just might want to use.

00:12:04.930 --> 00:12:07.120
And you might want to
deploy them to the Cloud.

00:12:07.120 --> 00:12:09.960
So we have a service called
TensorFlow Hub, which

00:12:09.960 --> 00:12:13.410
is on tfhub.dev, where
the idea is that that's

00:12:13.410 --> 00:12:15.900
a repository of
pretrained models

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:19.020
that might break out of the
scenarios that we're hosting

00:12:19.020 --> 00:12:20.880
and allow you to have
a model that then you

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:22.690
can host in the Cloud.

00:12:22.690 --> 00:12:25.440
The second scenario
is retraining models.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:26.850
Now earlier, I
mentioned the fact

00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:29.880
that there's those three
pillars in my previous slide

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:32.220
that you could retrain
those for your scenario.

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:34.050
But what if you have
a scenario where

00:12:34.050 --> 00:12:37.200
there's an existing model,
and you want to retrain that?

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:39.390
So for example, that may
be a computer vision model

00:12:39.390 --> 00:12:41.015
that you want to
retrain, but you don't

00:12:41.015 --> 00:12:42.600
want to host it with AutoML.

00:12:42.600 --> 00:12:43.913
Well, that's also possible.

00:12:43.913 --> 00:12:45.330
You'll be able to
take a model out

00:12:45.330 --> 00:12:46.740
of something like
TensorFlow Hub.

00:12:46.740 --> 00:12:48.000
And you'll be able
to retrain that.

00:12:48.000 --> 00:12:49.730
And you'll be able
to host it yourself.

00:12:49.730 --> 00:12:51.910
And I'll talk about hosting
it yourself in a moment.

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:54.388
And then, of course, there's
building from scratch.

00:12:54.388 --> 00:12:55.680
You want to start from nothing.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:57.472
You don't want to use
anybody else's model.

00:12:57.472 --> 00:13:00.090
You have a scenario that is
so particular and so unique

00:13:00.090 --> 00:13:01.033
to you.

00:13:01.033 --> 00:13:02.700
And that's what
TensorFlow is all about.

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:04.542
So TensorFlow is
your API that will

00:13:04.542 --> 00:13:06.000
allow you to define
neural networks

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:09.110
and train them so that
you can build a model.

00:13:09.110 --> 00:13:09.610
OK.

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:11.470
So then you've built
a model, and you

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:13.340
want to host it in
the cloud yourself.

00:13:13.340 --> 00:13:15.013
But you don't want
to use Google Cloud.

00:13:15.013 --> 00:13:16.930
Maybe you want to host
it on your own premise.

00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:19.640
Maybe you want to host it
in another cloud provider.

00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:22.103
So we have something
called TensorFlow extended.

00:13:22.103 --> 00:13:23.770
Now, the idea behind
TensorFlow extended

00:13:23.770 --> 00:13:28.120
is it's end-to-end, enterprise
type for building, managing,

00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:29.215
and running models.

00:13:29.215 --> 00:13:31.090
But the orange box that
I've highlighted here

00:13:31.090 --> 00:13:33.380
is a technology called
TensorFlow Serving.

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:36.370
And you could just use
TensorFlow Serving from TFX.

00:13:36.370 --> 00:13:39.100
Deploy TensorFlow
Serving to your server

00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:42.080
or to another cloud provider
or wherever you want to put it.

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:44.350
And then, you can serve
models out of that.

00:13:44.350 --> 00:13:46.690
So we want to just try
to be able to cover

00:13:46.690 --> 00:13:49.060
every base that we
can cover if you want

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:51.007
to run Cloud-based inference.

00:13:51.007 --> 00:13:52.340
So there's really three pillars.

00:13:52.340 --> 00:13:54.700
Number one, you can use
a service from Google

00:13:54.700 --> 00:13:56.840
and either use that
directly or retrain it.

00:13:56.840 --> 00:13:58.900
Number two, you can
build your own model

00:13:58.900 --> 00:13:59.845
and host it on Google.

00:13:59.845 --> 00:14:01.720
Or number three, you
can build your own model

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:03.250
and host it anywhere you like.

00:14:03.250 --> 00:14:04.840
So those are the
three main scenarios

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:05.920
that we're trying to hit.

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:09.400
So AutoML for the first,
TensorFlow Serving

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:11.350
on Google Cloud for
the second, TensorFlow

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:13.870
Serving on your own
server or in another cloud

00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:15.490
provider it for the third.

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:18.580
And if you want to learn more
about TensorFlow Extended, TFX,

00:14:18.580 --> 00:14:21.400
you can go to
github.com/tensorflow/tfx.

00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:22.653
It is open-sourced.

00:14:22.653 --> 00:14:24.070
And we have our
website around it,

00:14:24.070 --> 00:14:27.350
which is tensorflow.org/tfx.

00:14:27.350 --> 00:14:29.970
So next up is
on-device inference.

00:14:29.970 --> 00:14:31.750
Now, the idea is that
Abraham mentioned,

00:14:31.750 --> 00:14:34.250
your choice where you're going
to run your inference, number

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:35.558
one, you can run on the Cloud.

00:14:35.558 --> 00:14:37.850
Or number two, there was a
bunch of scenarios where you

00:14:37.850 --> 00:14:39.470
may want to run it on-device.

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:41.600
Maybe you don't
want to pass data up

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:43.250
to a cloud to have it inferred.

00:14:43.250 --> 00:14:44.810
You want to do
everything locally.

00:14:44.810 --> 00:14:46.910
There's a great video
out there, for example,

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:51.860
of farms in Tanzania and
Africa who rely on a cassava

00:14:51.860 --> 00:14:53.667
plant for their food.

00:14:53.667 --> 00:14:55.250
And the problem with
the cassava plant

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:58.520
is that when it gets diseased,
you don't recognize the disease

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:00.030
until it's too late.

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:02.360
But they actually trained
a TensorFlow-based model

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:04.550
that runs on-device
that can actually

00:15:04.550 --> 00:15:07.130
spot the disease in a plant
before the human eye can

00:15:07.130 --> 00:15:07.953
spot the disease.

00:15:07.953 --> 00:15:10.370
And all they have to do is
hold their phone over it, point

00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:12.080
the camera at the
leaf of this plant,

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:15.215
and it spots the disease
with 99% plus accuracy.

00:15:15.215 --> 00:15:17.090
And as a result, they
can root that plant out

00:15:17.090 --> 00:15:19.040
before it infects
the rest of the crop.

00:15:19.040 --> 00:15:20.498
Now, this is on a
farm in Tanzania,

00:15:20.498 --> 00:15:22.373
where they don't have
broadband connectivity.

00:15:22.373 --> 00:15:24.090
They don't have 4G
et cetera, et cetera.

00:15:24.090 --> 00:15:25.940
So everything has
to run on-device.

00:15:25.940 --> 00:15:28.640
So that's just a great example
of there are scenarios where

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:30.830
you may want to run
on-device beyond just privacy

00:15:30.830 --> 00:15:32.520
and all that kind of thing.

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:34.710
So when it comes to
models on device,

00:15:34.710 --> 00:15:36.760
it's actually the
same three choices.

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:38.430
Number one, pretrained models--

00:15:38.430 --> 00:15:40.430
in the example of the cassava--

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:40.950
sorry.

00:15:40.950 --> 00:15:43.070
A pretrained model is
you can go to TF Hub.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:44.570
You can download a
pretrained model.

00:15:44.570 --> 00:15:46.190
And you can just
use that directly.

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:47.720
Then, there's retrained models.

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:50.210
And in the example of the
cassava, what they did was

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:52.570
they downloaded something
called MobileNet.

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:55.040
And MobileNet is
a well-known model

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:58.370
that's optimized for mobile use
and recognizes 1,000 classes.

00:15:58.370 --> 00:16:00.440
But then, they
retrained that based

00:16:00.440 --> 00:16:02.960
on leaves that they
were able to categorize,

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:04.110
this is a disease leaf.

00:16:04.110 --> 00:16:05.400
This is an un-diseased leaf.

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:07.400
And again, using the same
thing that I mentioned

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:09.545
with the activity
detection, you can train it

00:16:09.545 --> 00:16:11.420
that this is what a
diseased leaf looks like.

00:16:11.420 --> 00:16:14.190
This is what an un-diseased leaf
looks like, that type of thing.

00:16:14.190 --> 00:16:16.670
So they were able to retrain
and build a model out of that.

00:16:16.670 --> 00:16:18.587
Or of course, you can
just build from scratch.

00:16:18.587 --> 00:16:20.745
You can create your own
model, and off you go.

00:16:20.745 --> 00:16:22.370
But when it comes to
on-device, there's

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:24.330
one extra step that
you need to do,

00:16:24.330 --> 00:16:26.600
and that's to convert
your model to optimize

00:16:26.600 --> 00:16:28.190
it to run on the device.

00:16:28.190 --> 00:16:30.090
What this does is,
it shrinks the model.

00:16:30.090 --> 00:16:32.390
It makes it not
battery safe, but it

00:16:32.390 --> 00:16:35.910
makes it better for working with
battery, those kind of things.

00:16:35.910 --> 00:16:37.290
So there's four steps in this.

00:16:37.290 --> 00:16:38.990
First of all, you
build your model

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:41.630
by either using a
pre-trained, retraining,

00:16:41.630 --> 00:16:43.330
or creating a
model from scratch.

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:45.290
And in TensorFlow,
then, you save it

00:16:45.290 --> 00:16:47.270
as what's called a saved model.

00:16:47.270 --> 00:16:50.330
So saved model is a file format
that we're standardizing on

00:16:50.330 --> 00:16:51.680
in TensorFlow 2.

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:54.620
And the idea behind saved
model is when you use that file

00:16:54.620 --> 00:16:57.590
format, it will work on
enterprise-grade scenarios

00:16:57.590 --> 00:16:58.250
in the Cloud.

00:16:58.250 --> 00:16:59.300
It will work on mobile.

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:01.092
It will work in
JavaScript, in the browser,

00:17:01.092 --> 00:17:02.030
that type of thing.

00:17:02.030 --> 00:17:03.655
To be able to get it
to work on mobile,

00:17:03.655 --> 00:17:06.530
then, there's TF Lite Converter,
which shrinks that model,

00:17:06.530 --> 00:17:08.390
freezes parts of
it, and allows you

00:17:08.390 --> 00:17:10.040
to use it on TensorFlow Lite.

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:12.200
So you go through these
steps for model conversions

00:17:12.200 --> 00:17:15.242
so that you can then execute
your model on a mobile device.

00:17:15.242 --> 00:17:16.700
And when I say
mobile device, we're

00:17:16.700 --> 00:17:19.550
actually moving beyond
just iOS and Android.

00:17:19.550 --> 00:17:23.060
The same process will be able to
be used so that your model will

00:17:23.060 --> 00:17:25.430
then work on embedded systems.

00:17:25.430 --> 00:17:27.950
So we recently released
support for microcontrollers,

00:17:27.950 --> 00:17:30.500
some microcontrollers
and embedded systems

00:17:30.500 --> 00:17:35.172
like IoT-type devices
like Raspberry Pi.

00:17:35.172 --> 00:17:36.630
So let me give a
quick demo of this

00:17:36.630 --> 00:17:38.410
if we can switch to
the demo machine.

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:40.500
And my screen saver kicked
in while I was talking.

00:17:45.070 --> 00:17:46.480
Thanks.

00:17:46.480 --> 00:17:47.090
All righty.

00:17:47.090 --> 00:17:50.740
So I'm actually going to run
this on the Android emulator.

00:17:50.740 --> 00:17:53.710
So we can see running on
the Android emulator now.

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:56.110
This is a little app that's
running in TensorFlow Lite.

00:17:56.110 --> 00:17:59.320
So it's an open source app
you can go and download this

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:00.240
now if you like.

00:18:00.240 --> 00:18:02.350
And what this is doing
is, it's grabbing frames

00:18:02.350 --> 00:18:03.730
from the webcam.

00:18:03.730 --> 00:18:06.790
It's emulating the webcam using
the front camera on my laptop.

00:18:06.790 --> 00:18:09.640
And then, it's classifying
those frames using MobileNet.

00:18:09.640 --> 00:18:11.290
MobileNet, as I
mentioned earlier on,

00:18:11.290 --> 00:18:14.330
is built to work with
1,000 different classes.

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:16.450
So what it's doing,
frame by frame,

00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:18.700
is it's taking the
frame from the webcam.

00:18:18.700 --> 00:18:20.680
It's compressing that
to the size of the image

00:18:20.680 --> 00:18:22.390
that MobileNet wants to use.

00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:24.100
It's passing it to
TensorFlow Lite.

00:18:24.100 --> 00:18:26.365
It's getting back
1,000 classifications.

00:18:26.365 --> 00:18:28.240
It's picking the top
three and then rendering

00:18:28.240 --> 00:18:29.510
those on the screen.

00:18:29.510 --> 00:18:34.390
So for example, if I hold up
a water bottle, we can see--

00:18:34.390 --> 00:18:36.590
I did say water bottle.

00:18:36.590 --> 00:18:38.040
Eh, there we go.

00:18:38.040 --> 00:18:40.445
It's hard to get it
right with the angle.

00:18:40.445 --> 00:18:41.820
I usually have a
banana for this.

00:18:41.820 --> 00:18:43.278
I forgot to bring
a banana with me.

00:18:43.278 --> 00:18:45.560
And we didn't have
a backup banana.

00:18:45.560 --> 00:18:48.730
There we go, water bottle, 100%,
so that that kind of thing.

00:18:48.730 --> 00:18:51.780
So if I, then,
just move this up--

00:18:51.780 --> 00:18:53.220
sometimes I put my face in it.

00:18:53.220 --> 00:18:54.900
And I don't know
what it wants to say.

00:18:54.900 --> 00:18:57.225
But so we can see the
inference time here.

00:18:57.225 --> 00:18:59.100
Now, this is running on
the Android emulator.

00:18:59.100 --> 00:19:00.700
I'm sorry if it's really small.

00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:04.232
With the inference time,
it's about 300 milliseconds

00:19:04.232 --> 00:19:05.190
on an Android emulator.

00:19:05.190 --> 00:19:08.040
If you run this on a device,
particularly an Android device

00:19:08.040 --> 00:19:10.687
that has a neural networks API
installed, like, for example,

00:19:10.687 --> 00:19:13.020
like a Pixel-- if you want
to see it running on my Pixel

00:19:13.020 --> 00:19:14.440
later, I can show you--

00:19:14.440 --> 00:19:16.770
it'll do the inference
in about 30 milliseconds.

00:19:16.770 --> 00:19:19.240
It's super, super, super fast.

00:19:19.240 --> 00:19:21.420
So we've been building
TensorFlow Lite with that

00:19:21.420 --> 00:19:23.940
in mind to shrink the
model, to optimize it,

00:19:23.940 --> 00:19:26.110
to make it super
fast for inference.

00:19:26.110 --> 00:19:28.230
So you can even do like
near real-time inference

00:19:28.230 --> 00:19:31.140
like this running on an emulator
on my laptop, which I think

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:32.220
is really cool.

00:19:32.220 --> 00:19:35.070
So that's the demo if we can
switch back to the slides.

00:19:39.530 --> 00:19:43.870
So in summary, for the
choices that you're facing,

00:19:43.870 --> 00:19:48.162
we realize that this is
a very confusing thing.

00:19:48.162 --> 00:19:49.870
So part of what I've
been trying to drive

00:19:49.870 --> 00:19:52.330
in Google is a bit of
a strategy around, OK,

00:19:52.330 --> 00:19:55.090
what do mobile developers
need for machine learning?

00:19:55.090 --> 00:19:57.832
And it was driven off of
a flowchart like this one.

00:19:57.832 --> 00:19:59.290
And I'm sorry if
it's really small.

00:19:59.290 --> 00:20:01.120
But that's kind of
part of the intent,

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:03.105
that we understand that
it's very confusing.

00:20:03.105 --> 00:20:04.480
But if we think
about this, if we

00:20:04.480 --> 00:20:07.090
look at the left-hand
side of this flowchart,

00:20:07.090 --> 00:20:09.340
those are the options that
are available if you want

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:10.745
to run inference on mobile.

00:20:10.745 --> 00:20:12.370
And if you want to
copy this flowchart,

00:20:12.370 --> 00:20:13.340
just drop me an email.

00:20:13.340 --> 00:20:14.740
I'm happy to send it to
you because I know it's

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:16.390
pretty small to see on here.

00:20:16.390 --> 00:20:18.400
And if you look at
the right-hand side

00:20:18.400 --> 00:20:20.410
of this flowchart,
those are the options

00:20:20.410 --> 00:20:22.910
that are available
to Cloud developers.

00:20:22.910 --> 00:20:26.077
So the idea is if you want to
run inference on mobile device,

00:20:26.077 --> 00:20:27.410
there are a number of scenarios.

00:20:27.410 --> 00:20:29.845
Maybe you want to have
it connected to the Cloud

00:20:29.845 --> 00:20:30.720
to do your inference.

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:33.190
Maybe you want it disconnect
it from the Cloud.

00:20:33.190 --> 00:20:35.482
Those are the things on the
left side of the flowchart.

00:20:35.482 --> 00:20:38.740
Ditto on the right side if you
want to use Google services

00:20:38.740 --> 00:20:41.570
or if you want to host your own
models, those kind of things.

00:20:41.570 --> 00:20:43.340
But we also have an
offering that Abraham

00:20:43.340 --> 00:20:45.850
is going to talk about
that gives us both.

00:20:45.850 --> 00:20:48.310
So the idea is-- and Abraham
probably will be best

00:20:48.310 --> 00:20:49.672
to explain it with ML Kit.

00:20:49.672 --> 00:20:51.130
LAURENCE MORONEY:
Thanks, Laurence.

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:53.140
As Laurence
described, ML options

00:20:53.140 --> 00:20:55.480
may look like a
[INAUDIBLE] at first look

00:20:55.480 --> 00:20:57.430
because you would like
to provide tailored

00:20:57.430 --> 00:21:00.580
solutions for each use case.

00:21:00.580 --> 00:21:03.220
As a developer, you want to
implement these solutions

00:21:03.220 --> 00:21:04.210
together.

00:21:04.210 --> 00:21:08.890
Hence, you can use the
ML Kit as a single SDK.

00:21:08.890 --> 00:21:12.650
ML Kit is a mobile SDK that
brings Google's machine

00:21:12.650 --> 00:21:16.130
learning expertise to
Android [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:16.130 --> 00:21:19.490
in a powerful, but
easy-to-use package.

00:21:19.490 --> 00:21:22.040
It comes with a set
of ready-to-use APIs.

00:21:22.040 --> 00:21:24.170
We'll call them base APIs.

00:21:24.170 --> 00:21:29.450
And they work for both on-device
and Cloud-based inferences.

00:21:29.450 --> 00:21:32.240
They support common
mobile use cases

00:21:32.240 --> 00:21:36.920
like image labeling, face
detection, texture cognition,

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:39.680
and barcode scanning,
to name a few.

00:21:39.680 --> 00:21:42.670
While they're all ML
models under the hood,

00:21:42.670 --> 00:21:45.350
they're as easy to
use as any other API.

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:50.630
Let's look at a typical
workflow of someone

00:21:50.630 --> 00:21:55.080
looking to implement a machine
learning model in an app.

00:21:55.080 --> 00:21:59.190
So first, the ML expert,
data scientist gathers,

00:21:59.190 --> 00:22:03.300
massages, and splits the data;
brings or creates a model;

00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:05.880
trains, tunes and
evaluate the model.

00:22:05.880 --> 00:22:09.180
Maybe they will have to
do this in a few loops.

00:22:09.180 --> 00:22:11.620
And then, the DevOps
will deploy the model.

00:22:11.620 --> 00:22:13.510
And finally, the
software developer

00:22:13.510 --> 00:22:15.720
will do the prediction.

00:22:15.720 --> 00:22:19.460
But ML Kit makes
this way easier.

00:22:19.460 --> 00:22:22.640
You simply pass in the
data to the ML Kit library,

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:25.430
and it will give you the
information you need.

00:22:25.430 --> 00:22:28.370
Whether you are new to or
experienced with machine

00:22:28.370 --> 00:22:31.190
learning, you can
implement the functionality

00:22:31.190 --> 00:22:33.170
in a few lines of code.

00:22:33.170 --> 00:22:37.200
And it includes both
Android and iOS libraries.

00:22:37.200 --> 00:22:41.640
While on-device APIs process the
data quickly and will work even

00:22:41.640 --> 00:22:43.830
when there is no
network connection,

00:22:43.830 --> 00:22:46.680
the Cloud-based API has
leveraged the power of Google

00:22:46.680 --> 00:22:49.050
Cloud platforms,
machine learning

00:22:49.050 --> 00:22:52.935
technology to give powerful,
high-accuracy results.

00:22:55.660 --> 00:22:58.050
Let's look at a
specific example.

00:22:58.050 --> 00:22:59.760
As you can see here,
we are building

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:02.400
an app that requires
identifying the teams

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:04.350
and content of an image.

00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:07.170
You would be using the
image labeling API.

00:23:07.170 --> 00:23:10.440
In the cart, you'll be
seeing two icons here.

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:13.200
On-device APIs
are free of charge

00:23:13.200 --> 00:23:15.570
and are best suited
for scenarios

00:23:15.570 --> 00:23:19.110
where you need a low
latency, and you might not

00:23:19.110 --> 00:23:21.090
have a network
connection, and you're

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:24.940
OK with a more
coarse-grained answer.

00:23:24.940 --> 00:23:28.630
On the other hand, the Cloud
calls come at a free quota,

00:23:28.630 --> 00:23:30.570
but are paid beyond that.

00:23:30.570 --> 00:23:34.910
They provide the most accurate
results and are powered

00:23:34.910 --> 00:23:38.330
by the state-of-the-art
machine learning models running

00:23:38.330 --> 00:23:39.935
on the Google Cloud Platform.

00:23:42.950 --> 00:23:45.680
But if you do have
some ML experience

00:23:45.680 --> 00:23:49.100
or would like to bring
your own custom models,

00:23:49.100 --> 00:23:52.220
as Laurence already described,
you can do that too.

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:54.050
And using ML Kit
with that, you'll

00:23:54.050 --> 00:23:58.130
be able to run your TensorFlow
Lite models on the underlying

00:23:58.130 --> 00:24:03.790
framework, whether
it's Android or iOS.

00:24:03.790 --> 00:24:06.850
And you'll get further
benefits from ML Kit

00:24:06.850 --> 00:24:10.960
since it's integrated into the
Firebase, such as analytics,

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:14.470
remote config, and A/B testing.

00:24:14.470 --> 00:24:16.700
Maybe not everyone is
familiar with Firebase.

00:24:16.700 --> 00:24:20.740
So Firebase is Google's
mobile development platform

00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:23.200
which has tools for
building better apps,

00:24:23.200 --> 00:24:28.340
improving app quality, and
further growing your business.

00:24:28.340 --> 00:24:31.530
So with using ML
Kit with Firebase,

00:24:31.530 --> 00:24:33.980
we'll actually allow you
to upload your TF Lite

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:37.580
models into the Firebase
console and dynamically

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:39.960
deploy them to your users.

00:24:39.960 --> 00:24:44.450
This means that the APK bundle
size can be really kept small

00:24:44.450 --> 00:24:46.400
since you won't be
actually putting

00:24:46.400 --> 00:24:48.860
the model inside the APK.

00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:50.840
And you can do
frequent roll-outs

00:24:50.840 --> 00:24:53.670
without having to
republish your app.

00:24:53.670 --> 00:24:56.980
And you can further
run A/B experiments.

00:24:56.980 --> 00:25:01.600
Firebase features, such as
A/B testing, remote config,

00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:04.670
analytics make this
process real easy.

